1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They power our lives.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
From cars to lawn mowers, from ships to steam trains.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
They've transformed our world and taken us to the moon.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Now, engines on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It's no accident that the root word of engine is ingenious.

6
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,540
For three centuries, these ingenious designs have been the ultimate expression of man's desire for technical excellence.

7
00:00:55,541 --> 00:00:59,580
and through many generations of technological progress,

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,140
they've had a profound effect on the way people travel and work.

9
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:08,440
Steam engines replaced the horse.

10
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:14,340
Electric motors and gasoline engines replaced the steam engine.

11
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:21,600
Then, the jet engine and the rocket engine took things to a higher level.

12
00:01:21,601 --> 00:01:27,880
and now microtechnology engines are proving that less is more

13
00:01:27,881 --> 00:01:38,680
this march through the centuries began when the steam engine ushered in the industrial revolution

14
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:46,640
it freed mankind from total dependence on primary sources of power

15
00:01:46,641 --> 00:01:49,500
like wind, water, and muscle.

16
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:53,340
Steam engines would be used to power factory machinery,

17
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:57,100
trains, ships, tractors, and automobiles.

18
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:02,100
Before steam, mostly we used animals as our mode of transportation.

19
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,920
When steam came along, of course, we could move more material faster

20
00:02:05,921 --> 00:02:08,900
and more reliably than we could, of course, with animals.

21
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:10,580
And so that was a big transformation.

22
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:13,260
People became less tied to being in small cities.

23
00:02:13,261 --> 00:02:17,280
they could live out on the farm and still be connected, if you will, to the cities.

24
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:22,960
In a steam piston engine, steam enters one end of a cylinder and pushes a piston back.

25
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:25,920
Then it enters the other end, pushing it the other way.

26
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:30,360
The steam comes from a boiler, a metal water container that is heated,

27
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:34,560
usually by burning fuels like wood, coal, oil, or natural gas.

28
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,500
A steam turbine engine is like a windmill,

29
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:42,980
except that its blades are propelled by hot steam under high pressure instead of wind.

30
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:46,960
To this day, steam turbines are widely used.

31
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:49,780
In fact, they are used to generate most of our electricity.

32
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:59,060
The Greek scientist Hiro of Alexandria built the world's first steam engine about 2,000 years ago.

33
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:05,060
It was basically just a round metal ball with two kettle-like spouts coming out of it.

34
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:08,980
When steam was piped into the ball, it spun around.

35
00:03:08,981 --> 00:03:16,380
Hero put it on display at the Serapium, a museum in Athens, where it entertained visitors for years.

36
00:03:16,381 --> 00:03:19,880
Although it was never viewed as anything more than an interesting toy.

37
00:03:19,881 --> 00:03:31,180
In 1705, two British engineers, Thomas Savory and Thomas Newcomen, produced a large steam engine that could be used to pump water out of flooded coal mines.

38
00:03:31,181 --> 00:03:38,180
The engine used a piston, a rod-like device that moved back and forth inside a tubular enclosure called a cylinder.

39
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:49,640
These early engines didn't have the gearing that could convert reciprocal motion into circular machine driving motion.

40
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:59,360
That didn't happen until the 1770s when James Watt developed what is probably the most famous steam engine of the 18th century.

41
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:08,620
James Watt, a professor at the University of Glasgow, developed several improvements for the steam engine, making it much more practical to drive machinery.

42
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:13,860
His flying ball governor expanded as the engine went faster.

43
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:18,140
Centrifugal force caused the heavy metal balls to spread out,

44
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:21,820
and that closed a steam valve which slowed the engine down.

45
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:28,740
And as it spread out, it would control the movement of steam into the cylinder,

46
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:32,160
and that was a very efficient device.

47
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:35,380
To keep it from revving out of control, but more importantly,

48
00:04:35,381 --> 00:04:39,100
to keep it in a steady, regular, uniform motion.

49
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:48,400
By 1800, more than 1,500 steam engines were at work in Britain, Europe, and the United States.

50
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:53,600
In addition to pumping water out of mines, they were also powering factory machines.

51
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:03,400
Britain was the first nation in the West and in the world, really, to become industrialized.

52
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:08,900
and so much of what was going on there was built around the advent of steam power.

53
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:13,400
By 1830, steamships were making regular crossings of the Atlantic.

54
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:17,960
The earliest steamships didn't look much different from the sailing ships of that period.

55
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:20,840
They still had tall masts and sails,

56
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:24,500
but the big difference was the large paddle wheel in the middle of the ship.

57
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:28,760
Turned by a steam engine, it provided extra power for the ship

58
00:05:28,761 --> 00:05:30,660
as it clawed its way through the water.

59
00:05:30,661 --> 00:05:43,720
Both steamboats and then later railroads dramatically shortened the time that people could traverse long distances.

60
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,005
The American politician John C.

61
00:05:47,006 --> 00:05:51,720
Calhoun often used the expression about conquering space.

62
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:56,600
And basically that's what these machines did, was that they helped to conquer space.

63
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:59,260
They shortened distances between two places.

64
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:07,300
That was particularly true of the steam piston engines riding the ribbons of steel that opened up the American West in the 1800s.

65
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:14,460
In 1860, there were more than 30,000 miles of railroad tracks in the U.S.

66
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,660
And in 1869, the Golden Spike was driven at Promontory Point, Utah,

67
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:24,400
to unite the Union and Central tracks which ran from the East and West Coasts.

68
00:06:24,401 --> 00:06:30,620
Most American trains at the time were pulled by so-called 440 steam engines,

69
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:33,560
which had four lead wheels and four driving wheels.

70
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:35,600
They weighed about 50 tons.

71
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:42,700
Another important application for the steam engine was in farm equipment.

72
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:46,240
Whether you're burning straw in the field with an agricultural engine,

73
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:49,800
whether you're burning wood, whether you're back east and you're burning coal,

74
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:54,440
You know, the steam engine was very adaptable and hungry for any fuel you could feed it.

75
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:56,820
And that made it ideal for farm use.

76
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:01,760
Huge steam tractors began to transform agriculture in the late 1800s.

77
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:11,860
That was an era when terms like behemoth, leviathan, some of those terms were very commonly used.

78
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:16,180
Some of these engines, you know, weighed up 10, 20 tons. Huge.

79
00:07:16,181 --> 00:07:26,420
In the mid-1800s, an American, George Corliss, developed the most important new steam engine invention since James Watt.

80
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:35,700
His new governor system allowed the engine to run more steadily, making it ideal for use in textile mills.

81
00:07:35,701 --> 00:07:42,780
This coreless designed engine recently restored

82
00:07:42,781 --> 00:07:46,880
was one of three installed at a sugar mill in Southern California in 1911.

83
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:52,960
For the next 67 years, it powered machinery that refined sugar beets into sugar.

84
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:57,900
They had steam readily available to cook and clean the beets,

85
00:07:58,440 --> 00:07:59,460
power all the machinery.

86
00:07:59,461 --> 00:08:01,520
It powered a generator in that factory,

87
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:04,100
so they had the lights and the power for everything else,

88
00:08:04,101 --> 00:08:09,580
and the motive power to run the centrifuge to run the sugar out of the beet pulp.

89
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:11,800
So, you know, very efficient system.

90
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:14,240
The steam engine just did it all.

91
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:23,660
The huge flywheel weighs 19,000 pounds, and the entire engine weighs 90,000 pounds.

92
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:28,320
The 300-horsepower engine was in service until 1978,

93
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:31,540
when the Holley Sugar Mill in Santa Ana was torn down.

94
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:37,600
A completely new kind of steam engine, one that had no pistons,

95
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:41,020
was pioneered in the late 1800s by two engineers,

96
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:44,760
Charles Parsons of Britain and Carl de Laval of Sweden.

97
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:48,860
And it's the only type of steam engine that is still in wide use today.

98
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,260
Steam turbines, which use steam pressure to turn fan-like blades on a rotor,

99
00:08:54,940 --> 00:08:57,200
are more compact than steam piston engines

100
00:08:57,201 --> 00:09:00,920
and usually permit higher temperatures and greater steam expansion.

101
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:03,180
That means more power.

102
00:09:03,181 --> 00:09:08,900
By the early 1900s, several steam turbine ocean liners were in Atlantic service.

103
00:09:08,901 --> 00:09:14,640
By 1920, the steam turbine had eliminated the older steam piston engines on major vessels.

104
00:09:15,420 --> 00:09:24,900
The great transatlantic liners from the Queen Mary launched in 1934 to the United States launched in 1951 were all driven by steam turbines.

105
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:26,800
Today, U.S.

106
00:09:26,801 --> 00:09:31,600
Navy aircraft carriers and submarines are powered by nuclear steam turbine plants.

107
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:37,580
The TV you're watching right now is in all likelihood being powered by steam.

108
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:44,820
Since the majority of electric power plants in America use steam turbine engines,

109
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:47,160
including nuclear plants like this one.

110
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:49,700
The power plant engine is indeed a steam engine,

111
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:54,260
whether, again, the heat source is a nuclear source or coal or natural gas,

112
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:56,220
it is a steam engine.

113
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:59,080
It's somewhat ironic that we still use a technology

114
00:09:59,081 --> 00:10:01,100
that was invented almost 200 years ago.

115
00:10:01,101 --> 00:10:06,260
The water circulates through the core, picks up the heat from the fission of it,

116
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:09,840
takes it to the steam generator where it flashes to steam,

117
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:14,360
goes out of the steam generator into the main steam pipes,

118
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:18,000
into the main turbine where it turns the turbine to make electricity.

119
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:21,380
Steam turbines may be going strong,

120
00:10:21,740 --> 00:10:25,300
but steam piston engines have long been silent.

121
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:29,200
They haven't been manufactured in the U.S. since the 1950s.

122
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:41,240
But the sound of the steam piston is all around at the Antique Engine Museum in Vista, California, near San Diego,

123
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:44,060
where they have dozens of working steam engines.

124
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:52,460
In their day, they were impressive images of unprecedented power.

125
00:10:53,140 --> 00:10:57,820
But there were inherent flaws that turned these fire-breathing monsters into dinosaurs.

126
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:00,040
Heat transfer is a slow process.

127
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:07,600
And in the steam engine, one needs to transfer heat from a hot fluid, namely the combustion gases,

128
00:11:07,601 --> 00:11:11,100
to the working fluid that actually produces the power, namely steam.

129
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:13,940
And people found that by using an internal combustion engine,

130
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:19,580
where the thing that does the expansion and the thing that generates the heat is one and the same material,

131
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:24,180
namely the fuel-air mixture, that one could get much more power out of a given size engine.

132
00:11:24,181 --> 00:11:31,480
But before the internal combustion engine came along, another new machine showed up to help power the world.

133
00:11:31,481 --> 00:11:34,180
It wasn't an engine, and it didn't burn fuel.

134
00:11:34,181 --> 00:11:39,180
Up next, the electric motor wins a place among the engines of the world.

135
00:11:39,181 --> 00:11:46,180
In the early 1800s, a good hand weaver could produce 48 yards of cloth in a week.

136
00:11:46,181 --> 00:11:51,180
A weaver working at a steam loom could produce seven times as much.

137
00:11:51,181 --> 00:11:55,260
Engines will return on Modern Marvels.

138
00:11:56,820 --> 00:12:00,180
We now return to Engines on Modern Marvels.

139
00:12:02,980 --> 00:12:08,800
When steam engines first came into widespread use in the early 1800s, they were extremely powerful.

140
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:11,260
But that power came at a price.

141
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:13,840
They were also extremely dangerous.

142
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:18,760
To create maximum power, their boilers had to contain steam at high pressures.

143
00:12:19,420 --> 00:12:21,040
They weren't always up to the task.

144
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:25,440
There were a lot of problems in the 19th century with the explosion of boilers.

145
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:28,200
Before you know it, you'd have an explosion that could kill everyone.

146
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:32,620
Robert Sterling, a clergyman in Scotland in the early 1800s,

147
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:37,060
was tired of seeing his parishioners getting injured or killed by exploding steam engines.

148
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:39,840
So he decided to do something about it.

149
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:44,420
Part inventor, part preacher, 100% Renaissance man. He was an incredible guy.

150
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:48,620
Sterling came up with an entirely new engine design in 1816,

151
00:12:48,621 --> 00:12:51,160
which he called a hot air engine.

152
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:54,420
Today, it's known as the Stirling engine.

153
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:58,260
The Stirling engines that he developed were low-pressure engines

154
00:12:58,261 --> 00:13:03,740
and so there was nothing really in there that was a high pressure that could explode even if the machine failed.

155
00:13:04,100 --> 00:13:11,280
Stirling engines are engines that heat one side of the engine and cool the other side of the engine.

156
00:13:11,281 --> 00:13:18,560
And then there's a mechanism inside the engine that moves the air back and forth between the hot side and the cold side.

157
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:22,880
When the air is on the hot side, it expands and pushes up on a piston.

158
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:26,400
When the air is on a cold side, it contracts and pulls down on a piston.

159
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:29,180
But there was a problem with Reverend Sterling's invention.

160
00:13:29,740 --> 00:13:32,940
The metals used in the 1800s were not heat-resistant enough

161
00:13:32,941 --> 00:13:35,760
to make the Sterling engine as durable as a steam engine.

162
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:42,620
The metals didn't stand up to the high-temperature continuous flame.

163
00:13:42,621 --> 00:13:48,940
The boiler is the part of the steam engine that is exposed to continuous flame.

164
00:13:50,540 --> 00:13:54,900
In a Sterling engine, it's the hot cylinder of the engine, so it's a different part of the engine.

165
00:13:55,220 --> 00:13:59,420
But with today's modern metallurgy, some believe the Sterling engine may now be viable.

166
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:06,760
Brent Van Arsdell manufactures small demonstration engines that show off the unique capabilities of Reverend Sterling's invention.

167
00:14:08,140 --> 00:14:11,360
One of the engines runs on the hot air from a cup of coffee.

168
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:19,320
But surprisingly, it also runs on the cold air from a bowl of ice.

169
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,480
All it needs is a temperature difference to make it run.

170
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:28,180
All you've got to do is keep one side hot and the other side cold.

171
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:31,780
You can do that any place that you can keep the temperature difference.

172
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:32,580
These things will run.

173
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:35,940
This Stirling engine can run on the heat from the palm of your hand.

174
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:41,460
Now, over the decades, people have tried to put Stirling engines in vehicles.

175
00:14:41,461 --> 00:14:50,700
And the conclusion right now is, no, it's an expensive engine, much more expensive than the sort of alternatives like gasoline, diesel engines.

176
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:58,540
And it's very hard to make it efficient on a vehicle because the way the engine is running changes all the time.

177
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:01,180
As you and I drive, we speed up, we slow down, we accelerate.

178
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:07,660
That makes it hard for the engine to stay efficient all the time, especially hard for the Stirling engine.

179
00:15:07,661 --> 00:15:14,840
The steam engines and sterling engines of the 1800s soon had a new competitor to help power the machinery of the Industrial Revolution.

180
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:18,740
And today that new competitor is still with us.

181
00:15:18,741 --> 00:15:23,740
We don't have steam engines in our houses, but we do have lots of electric motors.

182
00:15:24,540 --> 00:15:26,420
Look around your house. It's loaded with them.

183
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:34,600
Electric clocks, air conditioners, CD players, VCRs, fans, vacuum cleaners, blenders.

184
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:38,740
And of course, who could forget the electric toothbrush?

185
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:42,620
They are all powered by electric motors.

186
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:46,860
Without it, we wouldn't even be able to sit here and make this interview.

187
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:51,000
It wouldn't be worth getting out of bed, unless you wanted to be back in the horse and buggy days.

188
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:56,040
Unlike most engines, which use some kind of combustion to create heat,

189
00:15:56,460 --> 00:15:59,580
the electric motor is powered by an entirely different principle.

190
00:16:00,340 --> 00:16:03,740
It's based on the fact that when electricity flows through a wire,

191
00:16:03,741 --> 00:16:09,820
An electromagnetic field is created, which means you've temporarily turned that wire into a magnet.

192
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:13,500
Turn the electricity off, it's not a magnet anymore.

193
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:17,060
Hook it up the opposite way and its north and south poles reverse.

194
00:16:17,720 --> 00:16:22,020
An electric motor does this over and over, using the magnetic force to create motion.

195
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:25,140
What it does is it creates a magnetic field.

196
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:30,480
And it actually drives it, just like Doug and I are doing.

197
00:16:30,481 --> 00:16:38,880
As the power is going through this field, this is a magnetic field and it's revolving around here and it's sucking this around.

198
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:45,360
Of course, electric motors need electricity, so their history begins with the earliest electrical experimenters.

199
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:51,060
In 1824, Michael Faraday patented his direct current, or DC motor.

200
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:58,040
In 1888, the eccentric genius Nikola Tesla patented his alternating current, or AC motor.

201
00:16:58,980 --> 00:17:00,020
Today we use both.

202
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:02,660
If it runs on batteries, it's a DC motor.

203
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:05,380
If it plugs into the wall, it's an AC motor.

204
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,380
Tesla was the first one who introduced the concept of alternating current,

205
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:14,580
where you would change the polarity back and forth 60 times or 50 times a second as it was.

206
00:17:15,280 --> 00:17:16,660
And Edison didn't believe in that.

207
00:17:16,661 --> 00:17:20,100
And Edison actually fired Tesla and he went to work for George Westinghouse.

208
00:17:21,160 --> 00:17:21,920
Tesla was right.

209
00:17:22,240 --> 00:17:24,620
Alternating current could be transmitted over miles and miles,

210
00:17:24,621 --> 00:17:29,120
and there were significant limitations for direct current transmission distribution systems.

211
00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:33,540
George Westinghouse acquired the patents for Tesla's alternating current system

212
00:17:33,541 --> 00:17:39,900
and in 1891 installed the world's first high-voltage AC transmission line in California,

213
00:17:40,920 --> 00:17:44,140
connecting San Antonio Canyon with Pomona and San Bernardino.

214
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:51,260
In 1894, Westinghouse began manufacturing another one of Tesla's inventions, the AC motor.

215
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:55,740
Early electric motors were used to power some of the very first cars,

216
00:17:57,100 --> 00:18:00,100
along with steam engines and internal combustion engines,

217
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:04,400
which used an ignited fuel like gasoline to propel a piston up and down.

218
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:14,620
I don't think anyone knew, you know, at least for the first decade or more of the automobile's existence as to who was going to win out.

219
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:18,880
Around 1912, the internal combustion engine finally did win,

220
00:18:18,881 --> 00:18:21,800
Ironically, because of the addition of an electric motor.

221
00:18:22,600 --> 00:18:23,860
It was called the starter.

222
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:29,420
And it did just that.

223
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:33,440
Getting the pistons to start firing without throwing the driver's back out.

224
00:18:33,820 --> 00:18:34,520
Or worse.

225
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:39,440
Up until that time, you had to crank an engine to get it started.

226
00:18:39,580 --> 00:18:42,160
And it was not only difficult, but it was dangerous.

227
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:44,700
It could really break bones in your arm.

228
00:18:44,701 --> 00:18:48,000
If one of those things jerk back on you, you could really hurt yourself.

229
00:18:48,001 --> 00:18:58,820
But with the electric starter, that changed the whole ballgame and immediately signaled the demise of both the electric automobile and the steam-powered automobile.

230
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:07,660
Like the hand crank, the starter's electric motor initiated the compression and combustion cycles necessary for the engine to run on its own.

231
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:12,500
Some of the largest electric motors have been made for use in elevators.

232
00:19:13,420 --> 00:19:19,100
In 1933, Westinghouse built the world's fastest elevators for New York's Rockefeller Center.

233
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:27,300
In 1972, Westinghouse installed the elevators in what was then the world's tallest building, the Sears Tower in Chicago.

234
00:19:28,340 --> 00:19:32,320
In the 1990s, the electric motor made an automotive comeback.

235
00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:37,940
When General Motors introduced the EV1, it was extremely lightweight with a strong, rigid frame,

236
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:40,780
and one of the most aerodynamic car bodies ever made.

237
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:43,800
Some said it was built more like an airplane than a car.

238
00:19:44,640 --> 00:19:49,540
The highly advanced EV1 was a flop, and GM canceled production in 2000.

239
00:19:50,460 --> 00:19:55,340
As with all electric cars, its biggest problem was limited range between battery charges.

240
00:19:56,160 --> 00:20:01,200
If you're in a hurry to go someplace and you can only get 100 miles, have to stop 8 hours to charge,

241
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:03,780
I mean, you might as well have a covered wagon.

242
00:20:03,781 --> 00:20:10,880
I think it's unlikely that in the next 10, 20 years, electric vehicles will compete with standard cars.

243
00:20:11,080 --> 00:20:19,099
Battery technology is just not good enough, and these batteries are expensive once you take them up to the scale that you need to store enough energy to drive a

244
00:20:19,100 --> 00:20:19,800
vehicle.

245
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:28,674
Now, if they get a bit smaller and we're willing to have a limited range, it might be that electric vehicles will be interesting because they really would have no

246
00:20:28,675 --> 00:20:30,860
emissions where the car is being driven.

247
00:20:30,861 --> 00:20:34,900
Electric cars may not pollute when they're driven,

248
00:20:35,420 --> 00:20:39,220
but there's still pollution where the electricity is generated to charge their batteries,

249
00:20:39,660 --> 00:20:42,860
since most of it comes from power stations that burn fossil fuels.

250
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:47,440
Because electric vehicles require either batteries with limited range

251
00:20:47,441 --> 00:20:50,020
or overhead power lines they can hook onto,

252
00:20:50,600 --> 00:20:53,880
they've been more popular for public rather than private transportation.

253
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:59,880
Cities like Los Angeles got their first electric trolley buses in the early 1900s,

254
00:20:59,881 --> 00:21:02,180
And some cities still have them.

255
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:06,940
Virtually all subway systems and light rapid transit systems use electric motors.

256
00:21:09,980 --> 00:21:15,380
And just why do we call them electric motors when we call the other machines that power our lives engines?

257
00:21:16,340 --> 00:21:18,180
Well, that's a matter of debate.

258
00:21:18,620 --> 00:21:22,480
And it's a debate that can keep scientists and engineers amused for hours.

259
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:26,960
They sometimes refer to it as the great engines versus motors debate.

260
00:21:29,880 --> 00:21:31,640
I don't think there's a clear answer.

261
00:21:32,580 --> 00:21:38,540
Technically, we use the word engine for a device that takes energy from some source, like fuel,

262
00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:43,340
and converts it into power that we can use to drive something.

263
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:44,700
Engines versus motors.

264
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:50,560
In my personal opinion, I think that an engine, when I think of engines, I think of heat engines.

265
00:21:50,680 --> 00:21:53,000
I'm a thermodynamicist, and we talk about heat engines.

266
00:21:53,001 --> 00:21:58,900
There are many inconsistencies, even within the industry, with regard to nomenclature.

267
00:21:58,901 --> 00:22:04,060
The things that I would not recall motors as opposed to engines would be primarily things like electric motors.

268
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:08,240
And how do you explain outboard motors, which almost always have engines in them?

269
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:14,160
Very good question. I never said we were consistent in how we used those words.

270
00:22:14,980 --> 00:22:19,840
In the automotive industry, a motor is an electrical machine, an engine is an internal combustion machine.

271
00:22:19,940 --> 00:22:22,580
Then how did Detroit get to be Motor City?

272
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:26,160
Another very good question.

273
00:22:26,161 --> 00:22:31,800
Up next, the internal combustion engine creates a new industry in Motown

274
00:22:31,801 --> 00:22:34,300
And the world goes mad for motoring

275
00:22:34,301 --> 00:22:41,080
The first electric motor-driven appliance produced in the United States

276
00:22:41,081 --> 00:22:43,480
Was an electric pen invented by Thomas Edison

277
00:22:43,481 --> 00:22:45,920
It punched through the paper as it wrote

278
00:22:45,921 --> 00:22:48,520
Creating a stencil from which copies could be made

279
00:22:48,521 --> 00:22:52,420
Engines will return on Modern Marvels

280
00:22:52,421 --> 00:22:57,220
We now return to Engines on Modern Marvels.

281
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:01,560
At rush hour in a city like Los Angeles,

282
00:23:02,040 --> 00:23:05,880
it's painfully obvious how the internal combustion engine changed the world.

283
00:23:06,720 --> 00:23:08,480
Next time you're caught in a mess like this,

284
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:10,320
thank Etienne Lenoir.

285
00:23:10,820 --> 00:23:14,500
He developed the first internal combustion engine back in 1860.

286
00:23:14,940 --> 00:23:16,760
Like the very first steam engines,

287
00:23:16,900 --> 00:23:19,260
it was developed for pumping water out of coal mines.

288
00:23:19,820 --> 00:23:20,800
It was a big success.

289
00:23:20,801 --> 00:23:23,540
and about 5,000 of the engines were sold.

290
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:27,040
16 years later, in 1876,

291
00:23:27,800 --> 00:23:30,940
Nicholas Otto patented the first four-stroke version

292
00:23:30,941 --> 00:23:32,420
of the internal combustion engine.

293
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:36,240
Otto's four-stroke system is used in nearly all of our cars today,

294
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:39,260
so it's fitting that we call it the auto industry.

295
00:23:39,780 --> 00:23:41,820
We're still using that same four-stroke cycle

296
00:23:41,821 --> 00:23:45,140
and roughly our arrangement, we've got a piston in a cylinder,

297
00:23:45,141 --> 00:23:47,300
we've got a connecting rod and we've got a crank

298
00:23:47,301 --> 00:23:53,600
as a simple mechanism for converting the up and down piston motion to rotation of a drive shaft.

299
00:23:54,340 --> 00:23:59,640
That's what he came up with, and we're still using that today, 150 years later.

300
00:23:59,960 --> 00:24:04,160
In 1892, Rudolf Diesel patented the diesel engine.

301
00:24:04,660 --> 00:24:06,440
Note the absence of a spark plug.

302
00:24:07,100 --> 00:24:11,620
It's similar to the regular internal combustion engine, except that it has no spark plugs.

303
00:24:12,300 --> 00:24:14,580
Air is sucked into the cylinder on the downstroke.

304
00:24:14,581 --> 00:24:19,820
Then it's compressed on the upstroke which makes the air extremely hot

305
00:24:19,821 --> 00:24:24,540
So hot that when oil is injected the fuel air mixture ignites

306
00:24:24,541 --> 00:24:32,720
Mercedes-Benz made the first production diesel automobiles in the 1930s

307
00:24:32,721 --> 00:24:35,860
Because of their brute power and ruggedness

308
00:24:35,861 --> 00:24:41,540
Diesel engines made by various manufacturers are widely used in large trucks and heavy equipment

309
00:24:41,541 --> 00:24:50,260
As both gasoline and diesel engines evolved, they changed the way cities were built.

310
00:24:50,820 --> 00:24:53,000
They also changed the way wars were fought.

311
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:57,600
In World War II, U.S. engine production reached an all-time high,

312
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:00,580
as new engines made in Detroit powered the war effort.

313
00:25:01,380 --> 00:25:04,940
American auto manufacturers used their production machinery and know-how

314
00:25:04,941 --> 00:25:10,600
to build four million engines of all types and sizes, for trucks, tanks, and aircraft.

315
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:14,340
This mile-long factory outside of Dearborn, Michigan,

316
00:25:14,740 --> 00:25:17,660
cost the government $65 million to build.

317
00:25:18,760 --> 00:25:24,300
Under Henry Ford's management, it turned out 57,000 aircraft engines and 9,000 bombers.

318
00:25:25,240 --> 00:25:28,340
Ford's other plants produced a quarter of a million tanks and jeeps.

319
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:33,040
Since we were relying on piston engine aircraft for our fighters and bombers,

320
00:25:33,041 --> 00:25:37,260
the automobile industry converted its engine production facilities

321
00:25:37,261 --> 00:25:39,880
to producing engines for these applications.

322
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:43,580
And the new car production went down to very low numbers.

323
00:25:44,180 --> 00:25:51,020
Up until the 1950s, most engine development concentrated on making engines more powerful and cheaper to build.

324
00:25:51,580 --> 00:25:53,520
Then this appeared on the horizon.

325
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:55,180
It's called smog.

326
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:59,620
And because of it, engine technology had to accelerate in a different direction.

327
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:09,400
Pollution control systems introduced by all auto manufacturers in the 60s and 70s reduced emissions.

328
00:26:09,401 --> 00:26:14,400
but they also sapped power since they had the effect of reducing air intake.

329
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:17,700
An engine that can't breathe freely produces less power.

330
00:26:18,940 --> 00:26:22,740
In the life of the internal combustion engine, most developments have been evolutionary.

331
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:25,680
But one of them was revolutionary.

332
00:26:26,980 --> 00:26:31,280
German inventor Felix Wenkel came up with a radically new and simple design

333
00:26:31,281 --> 00:26:34,320
for an internal combustion engine way back in 1924.

334
00:26:34,321 --> 00:26:40,740
But it wasn't until 1957 that he built the first truly functional Wankel rotary engine.

335
00:26:41,500 --> 00:26:43,960
It was a dramatic departure from the piston engine.

336
00:26:44,900 --> 00:26:48,520
And because it spun around, instead of pumping up and down like pistons,

337
00:26:49,180 --> 00:26:51,760
the rotary engine dramatically reduced vibration.

338
00:26:52,660 --> 00:26:55,880
Many, many people over time have tried to think of a better geometry.

339
00:26:56,200 --> 00:27:00,020
There are some negatives to this simple piston, connecting rod, cylinder arrangement.

340
00:27:00,021 --> 00:27:06,580
masses move up and down and that's hard to prevent that causing vibration.

341
00:27:08,260 --> 00:27:15,280
But so far, with the one exception of the Wankel, nobody's invented a geometry that's got into real world production.

342
00:27:15,281 --> 00:27:22,440
This roughly triangular shaped rotor sort of moves around inside a container, but not quite symmetrically.

343
00:27:22,620 --> 00:27:23,400
It's off center.

344
00:27:23,401 --> 00:27:32,478
And so as it rotates this triangular rotor, it creates sort of smaller volumes and larger volumes in a similar way to the piston moving up and down in a standard

345
00:27:32,479 --> 00:27:33,380
engine cylinder.

346
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:38,580
I have a very strong attachment to the rotary engine without any questions.

347
00:27:39,780 --> 00:27:47,820
Kobi Kobayakawa, recently retired, was project director for Mazda's highly successful RX-7 rotary engine sports car.

348
00:27:47,821 --> 00:27:55,260
This is the only moving part of the rotary engine, and we don't have any intake or exhaust valve or camshaft.

349
00:27:55,460 --> 00:27:58,960
Basically, we have only two moving parts, two rotors.

350
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:09,020
In the case of a V6 engine, the moving parts like pistons and connecting rods and valves and camshaft, maybe 50 moving parts.

351
00:28:09,320 --> 00:28:15,540
In 2001, Mazda completed development of an all-new rotary engine model called the RX-8.

352
00:28:17,820 --> 00:28:25,320
It may look small, but the 255 horsepower engine is competitive with much larger piston engines.

353
00:28:25,760 --> 00:28:30,300
And it's still the only car engine in mass production that has no pistons.

354
00:28:31,240 --> 00:28:36,140
Kubi Kobiakawa talked to Felix Wankel just a few years before the inventor's death in 1988.

355
00:28:37,260 --> 00:28:40,680
I have been admiring him so many years.

356
00:28:40,681 --> 00:28:47,380
His eye to look into Mazda's rotary engine is always more like a father's eye looking at the children.

357
00:28:47,820 --> 00:28:57,140
He's so nice, and he was very, very pleased with Mazda's effort and result about the rotary engine.

358
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:04,620
Felix Wankel would be proud that the radical idea he came up with in 1924 spins on.

359
00:29:06,700 --> 00:29:10,960
Up next, tiny engines that make a dust mite look like a monster.

360
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:16,920
It's called microtechnology, and it's the next small thing in engines and motors.

361
00:29:17,820 --> 00:29:23,160
Today's auto engines are twice as efficient as engines of 30 years ago.

362
00:29:23,161 --> 00:29:28,800
They get twice the power out of the same amount of fuel and put out half the amount of exhaust fumes.

363
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:33,260
Engines will return on Modern Marvels.

364
00:29:34,040 --> 00:29:38,160
We now return to Engines on Modern Marvels.

365
00:29:45,180 --> 00:29:45,880
1941.

366
00:29:45,881 --> 00:29:52,380
The world was at war, and it was time for the next new engine technology to change the world.

367
00:29:53,640 --> 00:29:55,140
Enter the jet.

368
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:05,980
The jet engine is a gas turbine where you throw the exhaust gases fast out the back end to provide thrust.

369
00:30:06,980 --> 00:30:13,900
And the compressor and turbine of the gas turbine do the compressing to give you the high flow you need to throw out,

370
00:30:13,901 --> 00:30:16,480
and the turbine provides the work to drive the compressor

371
00:30:16,481 --> 00:30:18,400
and what's left is what you use for thrust.

372
00:30:19,140 --> 00:30:20,680
Imagine if you're baking cookies.

373
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:23,840
Imagine if you bake the cookies one at a time,

374
00:30:24,180 --> 00:30:26,940
you put some dough on a tray, you shove it into the oven,

375
00:30:27,160 --> 00:30:29,600
you wait for it to bake, and then you pull it out.

376
00:30:29,880 --> 00:30:32,120
That's what we do in the internal combustion engine

377
00:30:32,121 --> 00:30:33,280
with the reciprocating piston.

378
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:36,100
We put the fuel mixture in, stop.

379
00:30:36,400 --> 00:30:37,660
We compress it, stop.

380
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:39,580
Burn it, expand it, stop.

381
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:40,620
Push it out, stop.

382
00:30:40,740 --> 00:30:42,520
It's constantly stop, start, stop, start.

383
00:30:43,040 --> 00:30:47,740
Whereas with the jet engine, it's basically like a continuous process.

384
00:30:47,900 --> 00:30:51,040
Imagine putting all your cookie, don't want a conveyor belt, and running it through an oven.

385
00:30:51,620 --> 00:30:58,120
It all started about 60 years ago, when Britain and Germany were racing to develop the first ever jet aircraft.

386
00:30:58,660 --> 00:31:04,720
In 1941, Germany was the first into the air, with a fighter prototype, the HE-280.

387
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:09,700
A year later, the Germans had an even better jet fighter, the ME-262.

388
00:31:09,701 --> 00:31:13,680
and that was the world's first jet plane to go into mass production.

389
00:31:14,580 --> 00:31:17,740
Britain's first jet, the Gloucester Meteor twin-engine fighter,

390
00:31:18,120 --> 00:31:20,720
had its first test flight in March of 1943.

391
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,660
From there, we've come all the way to this.

392
00:31:25,540 --> 00:31:29,360
Airbus Industries is developing what some are calling the Super Jumbo.

393
00:31:29,900 --> 00:31:33,960
It's a double-decker plane that's 50% bigger than a Boeing 747

394
00:31:33,961 --> 00:31:37,960
and it's capable of carrying six or 700 passengers.

395
00:31:39,700 --> 00:31:48,160
In the early days of their development, rocket engines and jet engines were closely related,

396
00:31:48,640 --> 00:31:50,720
and people made little distinction between them.

397
00:31:51,440 --> 00:31:56,840
A rocket is similar to a jet, except that it carries its own supply of oxygen to create combustion.

398
00:31:57,980 --> 00:31:59,780
Jets get their oxygen from the air.

399
00:32:00,540 --> 00:32:03,360
Rockets get it from the oxygen tank they carry on board.

400
00:32:04,040 --> 00:32:07,500
That means a rocket can fly in space where there is no oxygen.

401
00:32:09,700 --> 00:32:14,840
Robert Goddard's rocket experiments in the United States in the early 1930s

402
00:32:14,841 --> 00:32:18,760
were followed by Werner von Braun's rocket experiments in Nazi Germany,

403
00:32:18,980 --> 00:32:23,360
which led to the V2 rockets which rained down on Britain during World War II.

404
00:32:24,560 --> 00:32:29,260
We knew that we had created a new means of warfare.

405
00:32:29,900 --> 00:32:32,820
The rocket technology pioneered by Goddard and von Braun

406
00:32:32,821 --> 00:32:38,180
enabled future developments like the breaking of the sound barrier by Chuck Yeager in 1947,

407
00:32:38,181 --> 00:32:41,500
the launch by the USSR of Sputnik in 1957,

408
00:32:42,080 --> 00:32:45,220
and the Apollo 11 landing on the moon in 1969.

409
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:50,580
Today, the three main rocket engines that power the space shuttle

410
00:32:50,581 --> 00:32:54,180
produce thrust equivalent to 37 million horsepower.

411
00:32:54,560 --> 00:32:57,040
That's as much as 23 Hoover dams.

412
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:02,940
Together, the engines consume 64,000 gallons of liquid hydrogen fuel per minute.

413
00:33:04,300 --> 00:33:08,060
The engines are movable and are used to steer the shuttle during flights.

414
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:15,520
They also provide additional thrust for launch, supplementing the two huge booster rockets which are jettisoned after takeoff.

415
00:33:17,320 --> 00:33:22,820
For decades now, advancements in the world of engine technology have led to bigger engines.

416
00:33:23,620 --> 00:33:29,260
But one of the most exciting areas today involves creating tiny engines that can fit on the tip of your finger.

417
00:33:31,500 --> 00:33:37,760
Microtechnology is a fast-growing area of research that has grown out of the miniaturization of electronic components.

418
00:33:38,180 --> 00:33:42,960
Some of the same manufacturing techniques are being used to build microtechnology engines.

419
00:33:43,880 --> 00:33:46,340
There are actually advantages to making things small.

420
00:33:46,341 --> 00:33:51,680
As you miniaturize something, the weight goes down as the third power of the dimension,

421
00:33:52,160 --> 00:33:55,020
but the propulsive force goes down as the second power of the dimension.

422
00:33:55,140 --> 00:33:59,480
So as it gets smaller, the actual propulsion to weight goes up.

423
00:33:59,960 --> 00:34:05,840
Professor Martin Schmidt of MIT has developed a turbojet engine the size of a postage stamp.

424
00:34:06,620 --> 00:34:08,040
And why did he want to do that?

425
00:34:08,180 --> 00:34:09,660
Well, first, it's awful fun.

426
00:34:10,860 --> 00:34:19,460
Second, there's propulsion applications, miniature aircraft, miniature satellites.

427
00:34:20,160 --> 00:34:25,000
Professor Schmidt's tiny turbo jet engine works exactly like the ones on a Boeing 747.

428
00:34:26,360 --> 00:34:31,580
This would be a turbine engine where air would come in through the center hole,

429
00:34:32,360 --> 00:34:36,900
and then fuel would be injected through a variety of these parts located here.

430
00:34:36,901 --> 00:34:40,140
and the air and fuel would mix after going across a compressor,

431
00:34:41,260 --> 00:34:46,980
enter a combustion volume that's in a circular region that surrounds this,

432
00:34:47,640 --> 00:34:53,020
and then go across a set of turbine blades and be exhausted out through this port here.

433
00:34:54,840 --> 00:34:57,300
And inside of that lamination is this little disc,

434
00:34:57,900 --> 00:35:00,440
and that's a disc that will spin at 1.5 million RPM.

435
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:05,600
Scientists at MIT believe this engine might power a tiny airplane

436
00:35:05,601 --> 00:35:07,800
with a wingspan of about three inches.

437
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:12,420
Hundreds of such inexpensive disposable microjet airplanes

438
00:35:12,421 --> 00:35:14,680
could be used for surveillance by the military

439
00:35:14,681 --> 00:35:16,460
or for weather exploration.

440
00:35:17,900 --> 00:35:20,760
Another application scientists have great hopes for

441
00:35:20,761 --> 00:35:24,040
is the use of these tiny engines to generate electricity

442
00:35:24,041 --> 00:35:27,860
so they could replace the heavy and less efficient battery packs

443
00:35:27,861 --> 00:35:29,580
and things like laptop computers.

444
00:35:30,520 --> 00:35:33,480
Some scientists are working on new microengine concepts

445
00:35:33,481 --> 00:35:35,560
that don't even exist at larger scales.

446
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:38,740
But again, the target device is about shirt button size,

447
00:35:38,840 --> 00:35:41,040
and we hope will generate about 50 milliwatts,

448
00:35:41,100 --> 00:35:45,080
which is enough to drive your cell phone or a personal organizer.

449
00:35:45,240 --> 00:35:47,800
You put a few of these together, you could drive your laptop computer.

450
00:35:47,801 --> 00:35:49,680
If we take a fuel air mixture,

451
00:35:50,320 --> 00:35:53,820
bring it into the center of a spiral heat exchanger like this,

452
00:35:54,180 --> 00:35:56,440
burn the fuel air mixture in the middle,

453
00:35:56,860 --> 00:35:59,040
and then as the combustion products go out,

454
00:35:59,200 --> 00:36:02,960
use those outgoing products to preheat the fuel air mixture

455
00:36:02,961 --> 00:36:08,420
that's coming in, you can actually get combustion under situations that otherwise the flame would

456
00:36:08,421 --> 00:36:09,121
extinguish.

457
00:36:09,122 --> 00:36:14,520
If we put devices called thermoelectric materials in these walls, we can actually use

458
00:36:14,521 --> 00:36:16,408
that to generate electrical power.

459
00:36:16,409 --> 00:36:19,240
Some microtechnology engines have gears the size of

460
00:36:19,241 --> 00:36:23,649
a grain of pollen and gear teeth the size of a red blood cell.

461
00:36:23,650 --> 00:36:26,280
If you want to make a microtechnology

462
00:36:26,281 --> 00:36:30,520
engine look big. Just put it beside a nanotechnology motor.

463
00:36:31,740 --> 00:36:34,440
Nanomachines are so small you can't even see them under

464
00:36:34,441 --> 00:36:38,260
a microscope. Nano means a billionth.

465
00:36:38,780 --> 00:36:42,480
So things that are a billionth of a meter would be nanometer, which is what

466
00:36:42,481 --> 00:36:46,620
is often discussed. Line up ten atoms in a row, and that

467
00:36:46,621 --> 00:36:49,260
row will be about one nanometer long.

468
00:36:50,580 --> 00:36:54,360
The roots of nanotechnology can be traced all the way back to 1959

469
00:36:54,361 --> 00:37:00,520
when the late scientist Richard Feynman gave a legendary talk at the California Institute of Technology.

470
00:37:01,080 --> 00:37:04,360
It was entitled, There's Plenty of Room at the Bottom.

471
00:37:05,460 --> 00:37:09,340
If we go down far enough, all of our devices can be mass-produced

472
00:37:09,341 --> 00:37:12,100
so that they are absolutely perfect copies of one another.

473
00:37:12,860 --> 00:37:16,860
I want to build a billion tiny factories, models of each other,

474
00:37:16,861 --> 00:37:22,140
which are manufacturing simultaneously, drilling holes, stamping parts, and so on.

475
00:37:22,141 --> 00:37:31,340
The issue was making things smaller and smaller, and that he didn't see any major violations of the basic physical laws,

476
00:37:31,380 --> 00:37:34,340
like the laws of thermodynamics, if you made something really small.

477
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:45,060
It is my intention to offer a prize of $1,000 to the first guy who makes an operating electric motor, which is only 1 64th inch cubed.

478
00:37:45,940 --> 00:37:48,120
The prize was claimed years ago,

479
00:37:48,800 --> 00:37:55,080
but Professor Ross Kelly of Boston College wanted to go beyond Feynman's challenge of 1 64th inch cubed.

480
00:37:55,680 --> 00:37:58,320
Coming up with a molecule, which is much, much smaller than that,

481
00:37:58,321 --> 00:38:02,220
is sort of the ultimate answer to his challenge.

482
00:38:02,500 --> 00:38:07,080
So it's taken us another 40 years to get down to the smallest possible scale.

483
00:38:07,520 --> 00:38:10,520
Professor Kelly succeeded in arranging 78 atoms

484
00:38:10,521 --> 00:38:15,340
to create a motor that consists of one single custom-built molecule.

485
00:38:16,080 --> 00:38:19,700
The original design, of course much smaller than this,

486
00:38:20,400 --> 00:38:24,880
had two parts, something that was going to rotate that looks like a gear,

487
00:38:25,120 --> 00:38:26,140
has three blades on it,

488
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:29,180
and something else that was going to function like the pawl and a ratchet.

489
00:38:30,640 --> 00:38:32,280
And it was supposed to rotate like this.

490
00:38:33,500 --> 00:38:38,040
Each corner represents a carbon atom with a hydrogen atom on it.

491
00:38:38,041 --> 00:38:42,500
it's connected to the next corner by a bond between two carbon atoms.

492
00:38:42,940 --> 00:38:46,280
Next corner, another bond between two carbon atoms.

493
00:38:46,480 --> 00:38:48,140
And because of the laws of chemistry,

494
00:38:48,680 --> 00:38:51,620
one can predict how long the distances are going to be

495
00:38:51,621 --> 00:38:53,600
and what the geometries are going to be.

496
00:38:54,340 --> 00:38:57,820
In other words, even though it's so tiny he can't actually see it,

497
00:38:58,400 --> 00:39:02,620
Kelly knows he's created a single molecule whose atoms function like a motor.

498
00:39:04,360 --> 00:39:07,560
It took him four years to develop his motor molecule.

499
00:39:08,040 --> 00:39:12,300
But now it can be produced in batches, large batches.

500
00:39:12,700 --> 00:39:16,260
Yeah, we had a flask with something like 10 to the 20th,

501
00:39:16,500 --> 00:39:20,120
approximately a billion billion molecular motors in it.

502
00:39:20,220 --> 00:39:23,420
You could make as many as you want, trillions and trillions and trillions.

503
00:39:25,060 --> 00:39:27,500
I think this will have an enormous impact.

504
00:39:27,620 --> 00:39:30,160
Being able to manipulate things at the micro and nanolone level

505
00:39:30,161 --> 00:39:35,100
enables us to build a nearly limitless number of devices and systems,

506
00:39:35,101 --> 00:39:39,440
and those devices and systems will have certainly as many applications

507
00:39:39,441 --> 00:39:44,520
as the devices we built during the Industrial Revolution did.

508
00:39:45,480 --> 00:39:49,260
Up next, cars that combine the best of two worlds.

509
00:39:49,940 --> 00:39:53,460
Hybrids powered by both a gas engine and an electric motor.

510
00:39:55,020 --> 00:39:59,460
In 2001, the U.S. government launched the National Nanotechnology Initiative

511
00:39:59,461 --> 00:40:02,380
with an annual budget of nearly $500 million.

512
00:40:02,381 --> 00:40:06,020
Engines will return on Modern Marvels

513
00:40:06,021 --> 00:40:11,380
We now return to Engines on Modern Marvels

514
00:40:11,381 --> 00:40:17,140
Hybrids, seen by some as the way of the future for automobiles

515
00:40:17,141 --> 00:40:20,540
are propelled by both a gasoline engine and an electric motor

516
00:40:20,541 --> 00:40:25,500
In hybrids like the Prius, introduced by Toyota in 1997

517
00:40:25,501 --> 00:40:30,060
the engine and the motor can propel the car either separately or together

518
00:40:30,980 --> 00:40:36,600
To reduce emissions, the electric motor is used to take the vehicle up to about 15 miles per hour,

519
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:38,880
and then the gas engine takes over.

520
00:40:39,980 --> 00:40:44,680
Electric cars put out no pollution, but the problem is they have to be charged up constantly.

521
00:40:45,720 --> 00:40:48,400
Hybrids offer some of the advantages of the electric car,

522
00:40:48,760 --> 00:40:51,360
except you never have to plug it in at a recharging station.

523
00:40:52,120 --> 00:40:55,160
That's because hybrids charge up their own batteries while they're moving.

524
00:40:55,340 --> 00:40:59,580
It's actually regening and putting energy from the tire through the motor,

525
00:41:00,060 --> 00:41:02,200
back into the battery pack.

526
00:41:02,201 --> 00:41:08,120
With regenerative braking, the car slows down by using its electric motor as a generator.

527
00:41:08,121 --> 00:41:12,280
This creates drag on the driveline and charges the battery.

528
00:41:12,281 --> 00:41:16,660
In other words, by functioning as a brake, the electric motor generator actually creates

529
00:41:16,661 --> 00:41:21,820
energy instead of wasting energy and heat loss like a regular brake.

530
00:41:21,821 --> 00:41:26,180
As we turn the corner here, initial acceleration is motor.

531
00:41:26,181 --> 00:41:29,700
And as I step into the throttle, the engine starts.

532
00:41:29,701 --> 00:41:32,860
Green is charge, red is power.

533
00:41:32,861 --> 00:41:38,320
As I lift off the throttle, engine shuts off, the energy flow is from the tires through the

534
00:41:38,321 --> 00:41:42,380
motor back into the battery pack.

535
00:41:42,381 --> 00:41:48,340
Toyota's competitor Honda began selling its two-seater hybrid called the Insight in 2000.

536
00:41:48,341 --> 00:41:51,840
Both cars sell for around $20,000.

537
00:41:51,841 --> 00:41:56,460
In Japan, over 34,000 hybrids have been sold since 1997.

538
00:41:56,461 --> 00:42:03,680
They're more expensive because you've got a battery and a motor to add to the engine that you're already paying for.

539
00:42:04,340 --> 00:42:09,240
And the benefits depend on the kind of driving these vehicles go through.

540
00:42:09,241 --> 00:42:17,920
So, for example, in Japan, they roughly double the fuel economy because it's very congested, slow speed driving, lots of stopping and starting.

541
00:42:18,200 --> 00:42:25,560
When we come to the kind of driving we have in North America, less congested, we drive at higher speeds, longer distances.

542
00:42:25,561 --> 00:42:28,040
then the hybrid's not as attractive.

543
00:42:29,760 --> 00:42:32,580
Trains have been using the hybrid concept for years.

544
00:42:33,300 --> 00:42:36,260
The locomotives we call diesels are really diesel-electric

545
00:42:36,261 --> 00:42:39,500
with a powerful diesel engine generating electricity

546
00:42:39,501 --> 00:42:41,580
for the electric motors that turn the wheels.

547
00:42:42,660 --> 00:42:46,740
But the hybrid isn't the only bright hope on the horizon for automobiles.

548
00:42:47,320 --> 00:42:48,980
There is also hydrogen.

549
00:42:50,020 --> 00:42:54,140
In 2000, BMW came out with the world's first production car

550
00:42:54,141 --> 00:42:56,400
run on either hydrogen or gasoline.

551
00:42:57,240 --> 00:42:59,260
When the car is switched over to run on hydrogen,

552
00:42:59,780 --> 00:43:01,080
it's like turning off the pollution.

553
00:43:01,600 --> 00:43:03,800
The only thing coming out of the exhaust pipe now

554
00:43:03,801 --> 00:43:05,100
is water vapor.

555
00:43:06,780 --> 00:43:08,740
But hydrogen comes at a price.

556
00:43:09,900 --> 00:43:11,300
The critical question is then,

557
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:13,400
all right, if you want hydrogen, where do you get it from?

558
00:43:14,460 --> 00:43:16,180
Well, the most economic way now

559
00:43:16,181 --> 00:43:18,560
is to make hydrogen from natural gas.

560
00:43:19,360 --> 00:43:20,920
Natural gas has got carbon in it,

561
00:43:20,921 --> 00:43:22,780
so in producing hydrogen,

562
00:43:22,781 --> 00:43:24,660
and we release the carbon into the atmosphere.

563
00:43:24,780 --> 00:43:28,180
That doesn't really help with the greenhouse gas problem.

564
00:43:28,880 --> 00:43:33,860
If we made hydrogen from nuclear power or perhaps solar energies,

565
00:43:34,080 --> 00:43:38,200
maybe we could find an economic way to do that

566
00:43:38,201 --> 00:43:42,500
that would let us produce hydrogen without releasing any carbon dioxide.

567
